{"name":"Hazel","hex":"#a36b4b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Hazel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from English from the plant or colour hazel. Popular in the U.S. at the turn of the 20th century.","A topographic surname from Middle English for someone who lived near a hazel tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Hazel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hazel","blocks":[{"text":"Hazels are plants of the genus Corylus of deciduous trees and large shrubs native to the temperate Northern Hemisphere. The genus is usually placed in the birch family, Betulaceae, though some botanists split the hazels into a separate family Corylaceae. The fruit of the hazel is the hazelnut; the trees are also grown as ornaments in hedges or gardens."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Corylus_avellana_0001.JPG/330px-Corylus_avellana_0001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazel","wikidata":"Q145889"},{"title":"Hazel (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Hazel is an American sitcom about a spunky live-in maid named Hazel Burke and her employers, the Baxters. The five-season, 154-episode series aired in prime time from September 28, 1961, to April 11, 1966, and was produced by Screen Gems, a division of Columbia Pictures. The first four seasons of Hazel aired on NBC, and the fifth and final season aired on CBS. Season 1 was broadcast in black-and-white except for one episode which was in color, and seasons 2–5 were all broadcast in color. The show was based on the single-panel comic strip of the same title by cartoonist Ted Key, which appeared in The Saturday Evening Post."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/05/Title_Card_to_Hazel_%28TV_Series_1961%E2%80%931966%29.jpg/330px-Title_Card_to_Hazel_%28TV_Series_1961%E2%80%931966%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazel_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q1266491"},{"title":"Eye color","blocks":[{"text":"Eye color is a polygenic phenotypic trait determined by two factors: the pigmentation of the eye's iris and the frequency-dependence of the scattering of light by the turbid medium in the stroma of the iris."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Farbverlauf_Augenfarben.jpg/330px-Farbverlauf_Augenfarben.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_color","wikidata":"Q23786"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG/500px-Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG/1280px-Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG/500px-Catkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young male catkins of Corylus avellana","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACatkins_Corylus_avellana-Mont_Bart-5124~2015_12_26.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%28ms%29_Corylus_colurna_6.jpg/250px-%28ms%29_Corylus_colurna_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%28ms%29_Corylus_colurna_6.jpg/500px-%28ms%29_Corylus_colurna_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/%28ms%